\title{MPI and MTI naming API}
\usage{
interval_check_loss(u, content = NULL, coverage_level = NULL)
interval_residual_product(y, eta1, eta2)
mti_constants(content = NULL, learning_rate = 1, coverage_level = NULL)
mti_loss(y, eta1, eta2, content = NULL, mean_tilt = 0, details = FALSE,
  coverage_level = NULL)
mpi_loss(y, eta1, eta2, content = NULL, details = FALSE,
  coverage_level = NULL)
tcsp_mti_boundary_target(...)
tcsp_fractional_tilt(...)
mti_posterior_draws(object, nd = NULL, seed = NULL, ...)
}
\arguments{
\item{u}{Numeric residual product.}
\item{y}{Response vector.}
\item{eta1, eta2}{Numeric root ordinates.}
\item{content}{Fixed interval content in \code{(0, 1)}.}
\item{coverage_level}{Deprecated alias accepted during migration.}
\item{learning_rate}{Positive generalized-Bayes learning rate.}
\item{mean_tilt}{Fixed response-scale retained-mean tilt.}
\item{details}{If \code{TRUE}, return component details.}
\item{object}{A fitted object.}
\item{nd}{Number of posterior draws.}
\item{seed}{Optional RNG seed.}
\item{...}{Arguments passed to the corresponding legacy implementation.}
}
\description{
The MPI and MTI naming layer provides standard public names for the mean-preserving
interval loss, the mean-tilted interval loss, fixed-target generalized-Bayes
fitters, TCSP helpers, and response-distribution Bayesian shortest-interval
procedures.
}
\details{
\code{mpi_loss()} is the zero-tilt specialization of \code{mti_loss()}.
The legacy \code{rqr_*} functions remain available during the migration period
so that existing scripts, serialized objects, and frozen evidence continue to
load. The new names are aliases to the existing implementations unless a
function needs the new \code{content} argument name.

The direct DP and Gaussian DPM functions intentionally use \code{dp_*} and
\code{dpm_*} prefixes because they are ordinary response-distribution models,
not MTI generalized posteriors.
}
\value{
The same value returned by the corresponding implementation function.
}
